// Plan 87's **pattern synonyms**: a pattern given a name and reused.
//
// Rust has no pattern abstraction at all. A pattern cannot be named, reused or
// parameterised, and `macro_rules!` does not reach the ergonomics — which is
// why the RFC has stood unaccepted for years. This is the largest piece of
// expressiveness in milestone 11 that costs **no type knowledge whatsoever**.
//
// The lowering is textual substitution at emit time, so rustc performs every
// check it would have performed on the written-out form: exhaustiveness, field
// names, types, binding modes. Doc 03 §2's corollary holds in both directions
// — Redox checks nothing new, and rustc checks nothing less.
//
// `pattern` is Redox's **second contextual keyword**, after `from`. Reserving
// it was tried and reverted: it is a *field name* in four of this compiler's
// own declarations, so `Keyword::PatternKw` made `redox-ast` stop transpiling
// with `RDX0103` on its own source. Two tokens settle the reading instead — the
// word, then an **upper-case** name, because a use site has to read as a
// constructor.

// The **nullary** form. A name for a pattern, with no parameters.

// The **parameterised** form. `a` and `b` are *formal* parameters: a use site
// writes its own binders and they are substituted positionally, so no name is
// introduced that the author did not write. That is the one property
// distinguishing this from `docs/DECLINED.md`'s refusal of `RecordWildCards`,
// which is declined for "introducing names absent from the source".

// A synonym in an **equation head**. The parameter takes a generated name,
// because an upper-case `Binding` is a constructor and not a binder — which
// plan 87 found by writing this line: before it, `describe Origin = …` emitted
// `fn describe(Origin: (i32, i32))`, a refutable pattern in argument position
// that rustc answers with `E0005`. Pre-existing, and nothing in the corpus had
// a bare constructor as a clause's whole pattern.
fn describe(__rdx_a0: (i32, i32)) -> &'static str {
    match __rdx_a0 {
        (0, 0) => "the origin",
        _ => "elsewhere",
    }
}

// A synonym in a **`case` arm**, which is the other position it has to reach.
// `Both l r` substitutes `l` and `r` into `(Some a, Some b)`, giving
// `(Some(l), Some(r))` — and `l` and `r` are bound here, at the use site.
fn added(pair: (Option<i32>, Option<i32>)) -> i32 {
    match pair {
        (Some(l), Some(r)) => l + r,
        _ => 0,
    }
}

// A synonym **nested inside another pattern**, so the expansion has to be
// recursive rather than top-level only.
fn holds_origin(slot: Option<(i32, i32)>) -> bool {
    match slot {
        Some((0, 0)) => true,
        _ => false,
    }
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", describe((0, 0)));
    println!("{}", describe((1, 2)));
    println!("{}", added((Some(3), Some(4))));
    println!("{}", added((None, Some(4))));
    println!("{}", holds_origin(Some((0, 0))));
    ();
}
